<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    The protocol between Bloom and the Freeze Doctor: the shared-memory layout, the session file, and
    the named events. Nothing else — no detection logic, no Windows probing, no reports.

    "Protocol" rather than "Contract" because it is more than shared data: alongside the layouts it
    defines the kernel object naming scheme and the signalling handshakes (the Doctor announcing that it
    is watching, asking a stuck Bloom to exit, the dump exchange). Those are conversation, not data.

    IT IS ITS OWN PROJECT SO THAT IT CAN BE ITS OWN PACKAGE. Both programs have to agree about this
    wire format exactly, and until now they agreed by holding two hand-maintained copies of the same
    750 lines. They drifted. Publishing one assembly and letting BloomDesktop reference it makes
    agreement structural instead of clerical.

    Deliberately kept dependency-free. Every type here uses only the BCL (MemoryMappedFiles,
    System.Text.Json, IO, Threading), so a consumer takes on no transitive dependencies at all by
    referencing it — which is what makes it a reasonable thing to add to Bloom.

    NOTHING IS PUBLISHED YET. `dotnet pack` produces the package and CI keeps it as a build artifact,
    so the shape can be reviewed, but there is no push step: a push to nuget.org cannot be undone.
    See PLAN.md and BL-16719.
  -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      net8.0-windows, not net8.0. The named kernel objects this uses (a named EventWaitHandle, a
      memory-mapped file in the Local\ namespace) are annotated as Windows-only in .NET, so a plain
      net8.0 target reports every use as a platform warning — and this project treats warnings as
      errors. Both consumers are net8.0-windows, and the Doctor is a Windows tool, so nothing is lost
      by being honest about it.
    -->
    <TargetFramework>net8.0-windows</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>BloomBooks.FreezeDoctor.Protocol</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>BloomBooks.FreezeDoctor.Protocol</AssemblyName>
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <!--
      No PlatformTarget: unlike the Doctor's own executable this is AnyCPU on purpose, so a consumer
      decides its own bitness. Bloom is x64; pinning x64 here would only get in the way if that ever
      changed.
    -->
  </PropertyGroup>

  <PropertyGroup>
    <!--
      Package identity. The `BloomBooks.` prefix follows the house style already in use: the Velopack
      fork at BloomBooks/velopack-l10n-fork publishes `BloomBooks.Velopack` and
      `BloomBooks.Velopack.Cli`, which Bloom references the same way. Deliberately NOT a `SIL.` prefix,
      which is likely a reserved prefix on nuget.org and would need the reservation holder involved.

      DECIDED (John, and this is the instruction for whoever wires up publishing): push to
      **nuget.org, then unlist the version**. Unlisted means it does not appear in search or browse,
      but still restores when a consumer pins an exact version — which Bloom does. So it is out of
      sight without anybody needing feed credentials.

      A private feed was considered and rejected for two reasons. It hides nothing, because this repo
      is public MIT and the package is just a compiled copy of source anyone can already read. And it
      would cost credentials on every developer machine, in TeamCity, and in GitHub Actions — for .NET,
      GitHub Packages needs authentication even for public packages — which is precisely the
      infrastructure whose absence made referencing this a cheap thing for Bloom to do at all.

      The mechanics to copy are in that repo's .github/workflows/release.yml: `dotnet nuget push`
      followed by `dotnet nuget delete`, which on nuget.org UNLISTS a version rather than removing it.
      Note that pushing a brand-new package id needs an API key scoped to "Push new packages and
      package versions" — their workflow carries a catch block saying exactly that, so they hit it.
      The key is the `BLOOMBOOKS_NUGET` secret.

      Still true regardless: a version pushed to nuget.org cannot be deleted, and the id is claimed for
      good. Hence review first.
    -->
    <PackageId>BloomBooks.FreezeDoctor.Protocol</PackageId>
    <Version>0.1.0-alpha</Version>
    <Authors>SIL Global</Authors>
    <Company>SIL Global</Company>
    <Description>
      The protocol between Bloom and the Bloom Freeze Doctor: the shared-memory health page, the
      session file, and the named events they use to reach each other. Referenced by both sides so that
      one wire format has one definition. Published for Bloom's own use and unlisted; not intended as a
      general-purpose library.
    </Description>
    <PackageLicenseExpression>MIT</PackageLicenseExpression>
    <PackageProjectUrl>https://github.com/BloomBooks/bloom-freeze-doctor</PackageProjectUrl>
    <RepositoryUrl>https://github.com/BloomBooks/bloom-freeze-doctor</RepositoryUrl>
    <RepositoryType>git</RepositoryType>
    <PackageReadmeFile>README.md</PackageReadmeFile>
    <!-- So a stack trace from a consumer can be read against this source. -->
    <PublishRepositoryUrl>true</PublishRepositoryUrl>
    <IncludeSymbols>true</IncludeSymbols>
    <SymbolPackageFormat>snupkg</SymbolPackageFormat>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <None Include="README.md" Pack="true" PackagePath="\" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
